\section*{The essential singularity of the planar XY correlation length}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. The XY correlation length is claimed to satisfy $\sqrt{b_c-b}\log(1/m(b))\to A$ with $0<A<\infty$ on approach from the massive side.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} For angles $\theta_x\in\mathbb R/(2\pi\mathbb Z)$ on $\Lambda_R=[-R,R]^2\cap\mathbb Z^2$, use the free-boundary cosine law proportional to $\exp[b\sum_{\{x,y\}\subset\Lambda_R,\,|x-y|=1}\cos(\theta_x-\theta_y)]$, relative to product uniform angles. Count each edge once and omit exterior edges. Define
\[
 C_b(r)=\lim_{R\to\infty}\mathbb E_{R,b}^{\mathrm{free}}\cos(\theta_0-\theta_{re_1}),\quad
 m(b)=\lim_{r\to\infty}-r^{-1}\log C_b(r),\quad b_c=\inf\{b>0:m(b)=0\}.
\]
The thermodynamic limit precedes the separation limit. For $0<b<b_c$, the axis correlation length is $\xi(b)=m(b)^{-1}$. There exists $A_{\mathrm{XY}}\in(0,\infty)$ such that
\[
 \lim_{b\uparrow b_c}\sqrt{b_c-b}\,\log\xi(b)=A_{\mathrm{XY}}.
\]
This concerns the nearest-neighbour cosine model and approach from its positive-mass phase.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
The analytic activity map from the critical companions is followed on scales that grow with the inverse-temperature distance. Dual-height fluctuation estimates identify the scale at which the marginal flow leaves the critical regime, and the spin--height mass relation converts that scale into the essential singularity.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
